# An interpretive question marked by a grown-up: the drivers put the dying Dave back in his traces.
# Either view is right if it is argued from the chapter.
item reading.wild-dave v=1 skills=[reading.inference, reading.evidence, writing.composing] {
  title "Kind or cruel?"

  scene 35x25 {
    column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
      row top space=1 align=end {
        sledge s
        bookcast b who=buck pose=pull dir=-1
        text ask "In chapter 4 the drivers put Dave back in harness, though he is dying. Was that kind or cruel? Give your reason, with a few words from the chapter." width=17
      }
      writinglines lines lines=4 width=34 prompt=""
    }
  }

  check writing.by-eye look-for="A clear view (kind, cruel, or something of both) with a reason drawn from the chapter: the drivers say a dog can break its heart when it is kept from its work and that Dave should die in the traces, heart-easy and content; against that, he cries out with pain, falls and is dragged, and the sled runs over him. A few words are copied from the chapter." notice=["Says kind, cruel or both", "Gives a reason from the story", "Copies a few words from chapter 4", "Notices what Dave himself wanted"] ask="What would you have done if you were the driver?"
  hint "What does Dave do when he is taken out of the traces?"
  hint "What do the drivers say to each other before they put him back?"
}
